The BBS
=======

bbs is no longer installed setuid root.  If you are upgrading an existing
installation, read "Setting up the group" below before the first make install
- without it bbs will start, but it will not be able to write the news and
uucp spools or take a lock.


Why it is not setuid any more
-----------------------------

bbs is not a daemon.  A user connects, gets a login shell, and types "bbs".
It therefore already runs as that user, and it needs no privileges to be that
user - the setuid bit was there for one reason only: three shared directories
the connected user cannot write.

  TCPDIR/locks      lock files
  NEWS_DIR          articles and outgoing batches
  UUCP_DIR          the forwarding spool, one directory per system

Everything else bbs does, it does on behalf of the user who started it, and
for the user's own files - .newsrc, the WRITE and SOURCE commands - it used
to hand the privileges back with seteugid() around each access.  Three narrow
brackets in a program that spent the rest of its time as root.

A group that owns those three directories covers exactly the need, and then
nothing in bbs runs privileged.  That is worth more here than in most places,
because bbs parses messages that arrive over the air: the addresses in their
headers used to become part of a shell command line, and every bug in that
parsing was a root bug.  See SECURITY-AUDIT.md, findings W-03 and W-10.


Setting up the group
--------------------

Once, as root.  The name is up to you; "hams" is what the rest of these
notes use.  Substitute the NEWS_DIR and UUCP_DIR that lib/configure found -
lib/configure.h has them.

    groupadd hams

    chgrp -R hams /usr/local/wampes/locks NEWS_DIR UUCP_DIR
    chmod -R g+w  /usr/local/wampes/locks NEWS_DIR UUCP_DIR
    chmod 2775    /usr/local/wampes/locks NEWS_DIR UUCP_DIR

and then name the group when installing:

    make install BBSGROUP=hams

which does the chgrp and the chmod 2555 on the binary.  Pass it every time:
the install rule replaces the file, so group and mode do not survive on
their own.

The 2 on the directories is the setgid bit: files created in them inherit the
group, so a file written by one user stays writable by the next.  The 2 on
the binary is what gives bbs the group in the first place.

Users do not need to be members of the group - bbs carries it.  Do not add
them: a member of the group can write the spools directly, without going
through bbs.

If you leave the group out entirely, bbs runs as a plain program with no
special rights at all.  Reading works; anything that writes a spool or takes
a lock fails.


Store and forward
-----------------

    bbs -f system [-e]

still has to run as root, from cron or by hand, exactly as before.  Both
options check getuid(), and that check was never satisfied by the setuid bit
- it reads the real uid, which is the invoker's.  Nothing changed here.


What the shell escapes do now
-----------------------------

SHELL and PIPE start a program of the user's choosing.  Both fork and give up
the real uid, the effective uid and the supplementary groups before they exec,
so the program cannot get any of them back.  With the setuid installation this
was not true of PIPE: it only set the effective uid, leaving the real uid at
0, and a shell that finds real uid 0 can call setuid(0).  PIPE is a USER level
command, so that was open to everyone who could log in.
